WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.735
The second A1 lesson deepens the classification story by moving inside hadrons.

2
00:00:04.735 --> 00:00:09.469
A hadron is not the final answer.

3
00:00:09.469 --> 00:00:14.204
It is the umbrella family containing baryons and mesons.

4
00:00:14.204 --> 00:00:18.938
The safest distinction between those two is structural.

5
00:00:18.938 --> 00:00:23.673
Baryons are built from three quarks.

6
00:00:23.673 --> 00:00:28.408
Mesons are built from a quark-antiquark pair.

7
00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:33.142
This matters because charge can vary and familiar names can distract the learner.

8
00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:37.877
The worked example becomes much safer when the student stops asking what the particle is called first and instead asks how the quark cargo is packed.

9
00:00:37.877 --> 00:00:42.612
Keep this takeaway in view: Hadron questions become manageable when quark packing is read before labels or charges.

10
00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:47.346
Umbrella family means hadron.

11
00:00:47.346 --> 00:00:52.081
This is the broad category that still needs to be subdivided.

12
00:00:52.081 --> 00:00:56.815
Three-piece packing means baryon.

13
00:00:56.815 --> 00:01:01.550
Three quarks signal the baryon route.

14
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.905
This diagram is strong because it gives the hadron question a visible route map.

15
00:01:06.905 --> 00:01:12.260
Start with the cargo pieces, meaning the quarks.

16
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.615
Then ask whether the finished hadron contains three quarks or a quark-antiquark pair.

17
00:01:17.615 --> 00:01:22.970
If it is three quarks, it is a baryon.

18
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:28.325
If it is quark plus antiquark, it is a meson.

19
00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:33.680
The diagram also protects a common misconception: protons and neutrons are not elementary particles at this level.

20
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.035
They are baryons because they are quark-built hadrons.

21
00:01:39.035 --> 00:01:44.390
Keep this takeaway in view: Baryon versus meson is best read as a packing rule, not as a name-memory test.

22
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:49.745
Cargo pieces: Quarks are the building units used in this hadron lesson.

23
00:01:49.745 --> 00:01:55.100
Baryon route: Three quarks place the particle on the baryon branch.

24
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.931
Imagine a shipping system with two allowed packing rules.

25
00:01:59.931 --> 00:02:04.762
One route accepts crates with three cargo units.

26
00:02:04.762 --> 00:02:09.592
Another route accepts matched pairs.

27
00:02:09.592 --> 00:02:14.423
From the outside, two crates may both look like part of the same shipping family, yet the internal packing rule still decides which route they belong to.

28
00:02:14.423 --> 00:02:19.254
Hadron questions work similarly.

29
00:02:19.254 --> 00:02:24.085
Hadron is the broad shipping family.

30
00:02:24.085 --> 00:02:28.915
Baryon and meson are the two structural routes inside it.

31
00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:33.746
This analogy works because it makes internal composition more important than surface appearance.

32
00:02:33.746 --> 00:02:38.577
Keep this takeaway in view: Hadron structure is safer to read from internal quark packing than from external names or charge patterns.

33
00:02:38.577 --> 00:02:43.408
Family label means hadron.

34
00:02:43.408 --> 00:02:48.238
This is like the broad shipping company or umbrella category.

35
00:02:48.238 --> 00:02:53.069
Route one means three-piece pack.

36
00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:57.900
That corresponds to the baryon structure.

37
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.320
The core statements are short but powerful.

38
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.740
Hadrons are particles built from quarks.

39
00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.160
Baryons contain three quarks.

40
00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.580
Mesons contain a quark-antiquark pair.

41
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:20.000
Protons and neutrons are baryons, which means they are not elementary at this level of the model.

42
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.420
This is why using charge alone is unsafe.

43
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.840
A positive charge does not automatically tell you baryon or meson.

44
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.260
The packing rule is stronger.

45
00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.680
The structure decides the route.

46
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.100
Keep this takeaway in view: The strongest hadron answers start with the broad family and then narrow by quark packing.

47
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:46.520
Hierarchy: Hadron is broader than baryon or meson.

48
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.940
Baryon reminder: Protons and neutrons belong here because they are three-quark particles.

49
00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.360
hadron -> quark-built particle.

50
00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.780
baryon -> three quarks.

51
00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.200
Use hadron as the umbrella family and baryon or meson as the more specific classification.

52
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:10.123
A common mistake is to stop as soon as the learner says hadron.

53
00:04:10.123 --> 00:04:16.046
But hadron is only the first layer.

54
00:04:16.046 --> 00:04:21.969
The better pattern is to say yes, it is a hadron, and now we ask which hadron route it belongs to.

55
00:04:21.969 --> 00:04:27.892
Is the packing rule three quarks or quark plus antiquark?

56
00:04:27.892 --> 00:04:33.815
This two-step method is much more powerful than memorizing isolated examples, because it works even for unfamiliar hadron names if the composition is given or implied.

57
00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:39.738
Keep this takeaway in view: Hadron classification is strongest when it is read as hierarchy first, structure second.

58
00:04:39.738 --> 00:04:45.661
Step one means identify hadron.

59
00:04:45.661 --> 00:04:51.584
Use quark-built structure to place the particle in the umbrella family.

60
00:04:51.584 --> 00:04:57.507
Step two means read the packing.

61
00:04:57.507 --> 00:05:03.430
Three quarks or quark-antiquark pair decides the final branch.

62
00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:06.646
Try four checks.

63
00:05:06.646 --> 00:05:09.862
Are baryons and mesons both hadrons?

64
00:05:09.862 --> 00:05:13.078
Yes.

65
00:05:13.078 --> 00:05:16.294
What structure makes a baryon?

66
00:05:16.294 --> 00:05:19.510
Three quarks.

67
00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.726
What structure makes a meson?

68
00:05:22.726 --> 00:05:25.942
A quark-antiquark pair.

69
00:05:25.942 --> 00:05:29.158
And are protons elementary in this lesson model?

70
00:05:29.158 --> 00:05:32.374
No, they are baryons.

71
00:05:32.374 --> 00:05:35.590
Those checks are enough to settle most introductory hadron classification questions.

72
00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.806
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the hadron builder now has a stable structural reading method.

73
00:05:38.806 --> 00:05:42.022
Family check means both are hadrons.

74
00:05:42.022 --> 00:05:45.238
Baryon and meson are branches inside the hadron family.

75
00:05:45.238 --> 00:05:48.454
Baryon check means three quarks.

76
00:05:48.454 --> 00:05:51.670
This is the decisive packing rule.

77
00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:56.847
You are ready for the worked example now.

78
00:05:56.847 --> 00:06:02.024
Start by asking whether the particle belongs in the hadron umbrella family at all.

79
00:06:02.024 --> 00:06:07.201
If it does, move immediately to the packing question.

80
00:06:07.201 --> 00:06:12.378
Does the particle contain three quarks or a quark-antiquark pair?

81
00:06:12.378 --> 00:06:17.555
Use that structural answer to place it on the baryon or meson route.

82
00:06:17.555 --> 00:06:22.732
If a familiar name appears, use it only after the structure is clear.

83
00:06:22.732 --> 00:06:27.908
That order is the safest way into the worked example.

84
00:06:27.908 --> 00:06:33.085
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: hadron questions now have a strong hierarchy-plus-structure method.

85
00:06:33.085 --> 00:06:38.262
Step 1: Confirm whether the particle belongs in the hadron family.

86
00:06:38.262 --> 00:06:43.439
Step 2: Count the structural pieces: three quarks or a pair.

87
00:06:43.439 --> 00:06:48.616
identify the umbrella family: hadron.

88
00:06:48.616 --> 00:06:53.793
read the quark packing rule.

89
00:06:53.793 --> 00:06:58.970
The best hadron answers do not stop at the umbrella family; they always finish the structural split.
